.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt DEHEX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dehex
.Nd decode hexadecimal values as ASCII
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.ad l
.Nm
reads a list of uppercased hexadecimal bytes from standard input,
printing for each one an
.Tn ASCII
character of the corresponding code point.
.Nm
continues reading input until a blank line
.Pq or Dv EOF
is encountered.
It is not an error for input to be empty.
.Pp
Values greater than
.Li FF
.Pq 255 in decimal
are split into pairs of hex-digits,
such that
.Dq Li AAFF
is interpreted in the same manner as
.Do
.\" NB: To reduce the width of the space separating individual byte values
.\" styled using mdoc(7)'s “.Li” macro, format them using individual lines,
.\" instead of a single multi-argument call.
.Li AA
.Li FF
.Dc .
Leading zeroes are prepended to byte-groups of odd-length:
thus,
.Dq Li 9450F3B
and
.Do
.Li 0945
.Li F3B
.Dc
are processed identically to
.Do
.Li 09
.Li 45
.Li 0F
.Li 3B
.Dc .
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Decode and print the word
.Dq Hello
to standard output:
.Dl Nm echo Qo Ar 48 65 6C 6C 6F 2E 0A Qc | Nm
.Pp
Decode and print a UTF-8 encoded character
.Pq Li U+1F602 :
.Dl Nm echo Qo Ar F0 9F 98 82 0A Qc | Nm
.Pp
Enforce uppercase hex-digits before decoding:
.Dl Nm echo Qo Ar 48656c6c6f2c20 Qc Qo Ar 776f726c642e0a Qc \
| Nm tr Bo a-f Bc Bo A-F Bc | Nm
.
